00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.880
CARTER MORGAN: With almost
500 sessions in Next 2019,

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.500
it's understandable you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.560
So if you didn't see
Hanan Youssef delivering

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.500
"Choosing the Right GCE Instance
Type for Your Workload,"

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.600
then stay tuned because
here's the rewind.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.023
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.380
Working with a cloud provider
to match your unique needs

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.230
with their specific
capabilities can be tricky.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.440
It can be almost impossible
to find the right VM

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.300
at the right cost with
the right performance

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.490
in the right locations,
and that's not even

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:32.650
taking into account that
your needs and budget will

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.530
change as your project grows.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.750
There's got to be a
better solution, which

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.150
is why GCP, from
day one, focused

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:42.060
on taking a different approach.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.120
See, we believe in simplicity,
flexibility, and efficiency.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.490
It should be simple to decide
what instance types to use

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:49.900
for your workloads.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.480
You should have the
flexibility to scale up or down

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.830
or even change your
instances as needed,

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.860
and your cloud offering
should be efficient.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.340
You should not be paying for
resources you're not using.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:04.000
At GCP, we do this by offering
four types of VMs specialized

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.840
for your specific workloads.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.080
We've got general purpose,
compute optimized,

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.380
memory optimized, and
even preemptible VMs.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.090
So how do you actually pick
the right VM type for you

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.030
on Google Cloud?

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.490
For 90% of your workloads,
you can find exactly what

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.050
you need with just one click.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.600
Yes, one click because,
for most of your needs,

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.080
general purpose VMs are the most
flexible and simplest option.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.060
They're great for a
wide range of workloads

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.030
like gaming, databases,
web serving, and more.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.250
They let you upgrade for
more speed or storage,

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.320
create custom machine
types, and you even

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.050
get recommendations that tell
you when you can make changes,

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.680
like downsizing to a smaller
VM that will save you money.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.220
It's pretty cool.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.270
But what about applications with
more specialized CPU or storage

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:47.442
needs?

00:01:47.442 --> 00:01:49.150
If you need cutting-edge
processing power

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.280
for intensive workloads,
that's where compute

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.600
optimized VMs come into play.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.540
These offer the highest
performance per core

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.000
available on GCP
and are a great fit

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.090
for AAA gaming, high-performance
computing, and some types

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.790
of scientific modeling because
you get full transparency

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.352
to the underlying hardware,
tight consistency,

00:02:06.352 --> 00:02:07.810
and predictable
performance, so you

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.350
can bet your most intensive
application on these VMs.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:13.090
So if you're making
the next "Fortnite,"

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.230
this might be for you.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.210
Now, what about
memory-intensive applications

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:17.470
that deal with a ton of data?

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.130
That's where memory
optimized VMs come into play.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.880
They have a lot of
size, from one terabyte

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.830
to approximately 12
terabytes with more coming.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.380
These are a great
fit for things like

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.350
high-end or in-memory databases,
real-time and in-memory

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.700
analytics.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.200
These use cutting-edge
technology

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.900
to make it simple for you
to persist data, scale

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.440
up or down your application,
and access data at high speeds.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.940
And that brings us
to the final type

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.190
of workload, bursty
jobs where you

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:44.290
need to run thousands
of cores for a very

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.160
short time on the cloud.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.830
That's a job for
preemptible VMs,

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:51.190
our affordable, short-lived
VMs that run for up to 24 hours

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:53.960
and could be preempted
at any point in time.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.870
These are great for
fault-tolerant batch

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.550
or rendering jobs,
or simulations

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.650
where you need compute
resources but you

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.500
don't need the application
to run in real time.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.600
What's cool is this
feature is really

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.000
just a flag that you can add to
any of the previous VM types.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.150
And once you add this
flag, you automatically

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.080
get a fixed 80% discount, so
you get a predictable pricing

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.100
model.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.720
To wrap up, if you're looking
for simple, efficient,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.640
and flexible VMs
for your workloads,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.978
whether they're using
a lot of CPU or memory,

00:03:21.978 --> 00:03:23.770
whether they're for
batch workloads or even

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:26.110
something in between,
Google Compute Engine

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.420
makes it easy to
find the right VM

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:32.150
at the right cost with the right
performance for your needs.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:34.030
If you'd like to see
Hanan's full talk, which

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:36.280
goes into much more detail
about the technologies that

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.690
make each of these
VM types possible,

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:40.880
click on the link in
the description below.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.660
And don't forget, subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube channel

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.580
for more Next Rewind content.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:46.730
I'm Carter Morgan.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.480
Thanks for watching.

